bal500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 181, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 26, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 47, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "aiq", "title": "Fortune AIQ 50", "year": 2025, "rank": 33, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 186, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 59, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 38, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 193, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--aerospace-defense", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Aerospace and Defense", "year": 2024, "rank": 3, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 57, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 47, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 48, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 199, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 56, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 60, "itemDescription": "Weapons manufacturer Lockheed Martin had a disappointing year in 2022 as revenue and operating profit both declined, 1.6% and 8.5% respectively. In his 2022 letter to shareholders, CEO James Taiclet attributed the lackluster performance to continued \u201cpandemic impacts and supply-chain challenges,\u201d saying he expected the company to return to sustained growth in 2024.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>In early 2022, Lockheed Martin canceled a planned $4.4 billion acquisition of rocket engine manufacturer Aerojet, several weeks after the Federal Trade Commission sued to block the deal for violating antitrust regulations.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Later in 2022, the Chinese government sanctioned the company for selling weapons to Taiwan, barring Lockheed from importing goods or making new investments in China.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The armsmaker inked deals worth a combined $37.8 billion with the U.S. government, its biggest customer, for its signature F-35 fighter jet.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 185, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 55, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">2021 was a year of mixed fortunes for defense manufacturer Lockheed Martin. Net income dropped 8% to $6.3 billion, as earnings took a hit when the company had to fund a pension settlement charge of $1.7 billion during the third quarter. But Lockheed Martin was still able to bump up net sales for the year by 2.5%, largely driven by deliveries of 142 F-35 combat aircraft, up from 120 the previous year. The F-35 accounts for around one-quarter of the company\u2019s revenue. </span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 144, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The year 2020 was a time of growth for the aerospace defense giant. Despite pandemic-related supply chain disruptions, Lockheed Martin still managed to increase net sales by 9%, to $65.4 billion. The F-35 program, off to a rocky start, rebounded with the company delivering 120 orders over the year. The firm\u2019s production backlog also rose, sitting at $147.1 billion as of spring 2021. Also in 2021, Lockheed Martin agreed to acquire U.S. manufacturer Aerojet Rocketdyne Holdings for $4.4 billion, a move to advance its standing in the rocket propulsion field. Following a strong first quarter, the company pushed up its sales and profits outlook for the year ahead. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The year 2020 was a time of growth for the aerospace defense giant. Despite pandemic-related supply chain disruptions, Lockheed Martin still managed to increase net sales by 9%, to $65.4 billion. The F-35 program, off to a rocky start, rebounded with the company delivering 120 orders over the year. The firm\u2019s production backlog also rose, sitting at $147.1 billion as of spring 2021. Also in 2021, Lockheed Martin agreed to acquire U.S. manufacturer Aerojet Rocketdyne Holdings for $4.4 billion, a move to advance its standing in the rocket propulsion field. Following a strong first quarter, the company pushed up its sales and profits outlook for the year ahead. </span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 49, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The year 2020 was a time of growth for the aerospace defense giant. Despite pandemic-related supply chain disruptions, Lockheed Martin still managed to increase net sales by 9%, to $65.4 billion. The F-35 program, off to a rocky start, rebounded with the company delivering 120 orders over the year. The firm\u2019s production backlog also rose, sitting at $147.1 billion as of spring 2021. Also in 2021, Lockheed Martin agreed to acquire U.S. manufacturer Aerojet Rocketdyne Holdings for $4.4 billion, a move to advance its standing in the rocket propulsion field. Following a strong first quarter, the company pushed up its sales and profits outlook for the year ahead. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The year 2020 was a time of growth for the aerospace defense giant. Despite pandemic-related supply chain disruptions, Lockheed Martin still managed to increase net sales by 9%, to $65.4 billion. The F-35 program, off to a rocky start, rebounded with the company delivering 120 orders over the year. The firm\u2019s production backlog also rose, sitting at $147.1 billion as of spring 2021. Also in 2021, Lockheed Martin agreed to acquire U.S. manufacturer Aerojet Rocketdyne Holdings for $4.4 billion, a move to advance its standing in the rocket propulsion field. Following a strong first quarter, the company pushed up its sales and profits outlook for the year ahead. </span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": 46, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 182, "itemDescription": "Lockheed Martin launched to new heights in 2019, as revenues grew 11.3% and profits 23.5%, and its stock climbed nearly 50%. Particularly notable was the continued success of the F-35 program: Lockheed delivered 134 of the fighters in 2019, and won new contracts including a Pentagon buy of 478 of the advanced jets for $34 billion. That adds to a production backlog that now sits at $144 billion, much larger than those of competitors. Despite global headwinds in early 2020, Lockheed\u2019s operations haven\u2019t yet shown signs of a stall; the company in July announced Q2 2020 earnings that beat expectations. The company is now being helmed by a new corporate pilot: James Taiclet, who was formerly the chief executive of American Tower, a real estate investment trust. Taiclet succeeds Marillyn Hewson, the top-ranked executive on Fortune's Most Powerful Women in Business list in 2018 and 2019, who stepped down as CEO in June. She will remain as Lockheed's executive chairman."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 57, "itemDescription": "Lockheed launched to new heights in 2019. Revenues grew 11.3% and profits 23.5%, making it the most profitable aerospace company on the 500. Its stock climbed nearly 50%\u2014well ahead of the robust returns of the broader market. Particularly notable was the continued success of the F-35 program, which for a time risked becoming a costly boondoggle. After producing 91 of the fighters in 2018, Lockheed delivered 134 in 2019, and won new contracts including a Pentagon buy of 478 of the advanced jets for $34 billion. That adds to a production backlog that now sits at $144 billion, much larger than those of competitors. Despite global headwinds in early 2020, Lockheed\u2019s operations haven\u2019t yet shown signs of a stall\u2014with first-quarter net earnings up slightly year over year, guidance for the year barely lowered, and a fresh $6.1 billion Pentagon contract for Patriot missiles."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 197, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug